.TH ZSTD-SPLITTER 1 "2026-07-25" "zstd-splitter 3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zstd-splitter \- compress, split, reconstruct, extract, and SHA-256 verify tar archives
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zstd-splitter
.B -c
.BI -s " size"
.RB [ -e " engine" ]
.RB [ -l " level" ]
.RB [ -T " threads" ]
.RB [ -i ]
.RB [ -f ]
.I source
.PP
.B zstd-splitter
.B -j
.RB [ -i ]
.RB [ -m " manifest" ]
.RB [ -f ]
.I part
.PP
.B zstd-splitter
.B -x
.RB [ -i ]
.RB [ -m " manifest" ]
.RB [ -d " directory" ]
.RB [ -f ]
.I input
.PP
.B zstd-splitter
.B -v
.RB [ -i ]
.RB [ -m " manifest" ]
.I input
.PP
.B zstd-splitter
.B -E
.PP
.B zstd-splitter
.B -h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zstd-splitter
creates a tar stream from one source file, directory, or symbolic link,
compresses the stream with a selected engine, and divides the compressed stream
into parts. It can reconstruct, verify, and extract those parts.
.PP
Zstandard is the default engine. gzip, bzip2, xz, LZMA, lzip, lzop, and LZ4 are
also supported when their commands are installed.
.PP
Version 3.0 adds an optional strict SHA-256 manifest. Strict mode covers the
source content and tree structure, the complete compressed archive, and every
split part. It also validates the restored source tree after extraction.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B -c
Create, compress, and split one source operand.
.TP
.B -j
Join all parts belonging to the selected part and publish the complete archive.
.TP
.B -x
Extract a complete archive. If the operand is a part, reconstruct and verify the
archive before extraction.
.TP
.B -v
Verify a complete archive or part set without extracting it.
.TP
.B -E
List supported engines, extensions, levels, and thread support.
.TP
.B -h
Display built-in help.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -e " engine"
Select zstd, gzip, bzip2, xz, lzma, lzip, lzop, or lz4 for compression.
Aliases zst, gz, bz2, and lzo are accepted.
.TP
.BI -s " size"
Maximum part size. Required with
.BR -c .
An integer may be followed by K, M, G, T, or P and optional B or iB. Units are
powers of 1024.
.TP
.BI -l " level"
Compression level. The valid range depends on the engine.
.TP
.BI -T " threads"
Worker threads for zstd, xz, or lzma. Zero requests automatic selection.
.TP
.B -i
Enable strict SHA-256 integrity processing. Compression writes a sidecar
manifest. Join, verify, and extract require and validate it.
.TP
.BI -m " manifest"
Use an explicit strict-integrity manifest with
.BR -j ,
.BR -x ,
or
.BR -v .
This option requires
.BR -i .
.TP
.BI -d " directory"
Extraction destination for
.BR -x .
The default is a directory named
.I name.extracted
beside the archive.
.TP
.B -f
Replace output without prompting. With extraction, an existing destination is
removed recursively and recreated. This option must be used carefully.
.TP
.B --
End option processing.
.SH STRICT INTEGRITY
The default manifest name is formed by appending
.B .manifest.sha256
to the complete archive name. For example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sample.tar.zst.manifest.sha256
.fi
.RE
.PP
The manifest records three layers.
.SS Source tree
Regular files are recorded with content SHA-256 and byte size. Directories are
recorded so empty directories and tree structure remain significant. Symbolic
links are recorded with a SHA-256 of their link-target text. Relative pathnames
are escaped for tab, carriage return, line feed, and percent characters.
.PP
The exact source records receive an aggregate SHA-256 named
.BR source_tree_sha256 .
During compression, inventories generated before and after archiving must be
identical or no output is published.
.SS Compressed archive
The complete reconstructed compressed byte stream is recorded with SHA-256 and
byte size. Reconstruction must match both values before publication.
.SS Split parts
Each six-letter part suffix is recorded with the part SHA-256 and byte size.
Strict reconstruction requires the exact number of parts and validates every
part before concatenation.
.SH EXTRACTION VALIDATION
Strict extraction uses a dedicated destination directory. After extraction,
the program inventories every top-level extracted object, recomputes the
aggregate source-tree SHA-256, and compares the complete inventory records byte
for byte with the original manifest.
.PP
The source-tree inventory does not cover ownership, permission bits, timestamps,
ACLs, extended attributes, sparse-file representation, or other filesystem
metadata. Unsupported special object types are rejected in strict mode.
.SH MANIFEST TRUST
SHA-256 manifests detect accidental corruption. They are not signatures. An
attacker able to replace both the data and its manifest can compute matching
new hashes. Authenticity requires an externally signed manifest or an
independently trusted manifest digest.
.SH OUTPUT FILES
Parts use names such as:
.PP
.RS
.nf
name.tar.zst.part.aaaaaa
name.tar.gz.part.aaaaaa
name.tar.bz2.part.aaaaaa
name.tar.xz.part.aaaaaa
.fi
.RE
.PP
Strict manifests use:
.PP
.RS
.nf
name.tar.EXT.manifest.sha256
.fi
.RE
.SH ENGINES
.TS
l l l l l.
Engine	Extension	Command	Levels	Threads
zstd	.tar.zst	zstd	1-22	yes
gzip	.tar.gz	gzip	1-9	no
bzip2	.tar.bz2	bzip2	1-9	no
xz	.tar.xz	xz	0-9	yes
lzma	.tar.lzma	xz	0-9	yes
lzip	.tar.lz	lzip	0-9	no
lzop	.tar.lzo	lzop	1-9	no
lz4	.tar.lz4	lz4	1-12	no
.TE
.SH EXAMPLES
Strict compression:
.PP
.RS
.nf
zstd-splitter -c -i -s 500M source-directory
.fi
.RE
.PP
Strict verification from any part:
.PP
.RS
.nf
zstd-splitter -v -i source-directory.tar.zst.part.aaaaab
.fi
.RE
.PP
Strict reconstruction and extraction:
.PP
.RS
.nf
zstd-splitter -x -i -d restored \\
  source-directory.tar.zst.part.aaaaab
.fi
.RE
.PP
Strict xz compression:
.PP
.RS
.nf
zstd-splitter -c -i -e xz -l 6 -T 0 -s 2GiB source-directory
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, help, engine listing, or an accepted interactive cancellation.
.TP
.B 1
Operational or integrity failure.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid syntax, option, operand, engine, or format.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
Base operation requires a POSIX-like shell, tar, split, and the selected
compressor. Strict mode additionally requires sha256sum, cmp, and readlink.
.SH PORTABILITY
The script is written for POSIX /bin/sh and uses getopts. Compression programs,
sha256sum, and several tar or split behaviors are external extensions rather
than utilities specified uniformly by POSIX.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tar (1),
.BR split (1),
.BR sha256sum (1),
.BR zstd (1),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR bzip2 (1),
.BR xz (1)
